<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ADDED 17 Oct 2026 - the unfilled remainder of a countdown ring.

     android:useLevel is stated EXPLICITLY, and the earlier reason given for it
     here was WRONG. It claimed the attribute's default differs across API
     levels, so that omitting it could yield a solid disc. On this platform
     GradientState.mUseLevelForShape defaults to TRUE (GradientDrawable.java),
     so a ring with no useLevel is still level-dependent - which is the more
     important consequence: the TRACK cannot be made "always full" by omitting
     the attribute either. It is stated on both layers so neither has to depend
     on a platform default that a different API level could resolve differently,
     and so the intent is readable at the point of the attribute.

     Geometry: innerRadius 16dp + thickness 6dp = 22dp outer radius, so a 44dp
     view contains it exactly. Explicit dp rather than innerRadiusRatio/
     thicknessRatio because those ratios are fractions of whatever bounds the
     drawable is given, which the launcher rather than this app decides - and a
     ring drawn at 52dp inside a 44dp view is CLIPPED, not scaled.

     CHANGED 20 Oct 2026 from innerRadius 18dp + thickness 8dp (a 52dp ring).
     The owner called the Next Dose ring oversized, and the stroke was the other
     half of the problem: at 8dp the hole in a 44dp ring would be 28dp, which
     is narrower than the "04:00" the ring's centre has to carry. At 6dp the
     hole is 32dp, which is exactly the width of that label.

     MUST match widget_ring_arc.xml exactly - a mismatch draws two concentric
     rings of different diameters rather than one arc over one track. -->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="ring"
    android:innerRadius="16dp"
    android:thickness="6dp"
    android:useLevel="true">
    <solid android:color="@color/widget_ring_track" />
</shape>
